\id 2TI Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Timothy
\toc1 The Second Epistle of Paul to Timothee.
\toc2 2 Timothy
\toc3 2TI
\mt1 The Second Epistle of Paul to Timothee.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE SECOND EPISTLE OF S. PAUL TO TIMOTHEE.
\ip THE chief scope of this second to Timothee, is, to open unto him that his martyrdom is at hand. Which yet he doth not plainly before the end: preparing first his mind with much circumstance, because he knew it would grieve him sore, and also might be a tentation unto him. Therefore he talketh of the cause of his trouble, & of the reward: that the one is honourable, and the other most glorious: and exhorteth him to be constant in the faith, to be ready always to suffer for it, to fulfill his ministry to the end, as himself now had done his.
\ip Whereby it is certain, that it was written at Rome, in his last apprehension and imprisonment there: as he signifieth by these words Cap. 1: \it Onesiphorus was not ashamed of my chain, but when he was come to Rome, carefully sought me, &c.\it* And of his martyrdom, thus: \it For I am now ready to be offered, and the time of my resolution\it* (or death) \it is at hand.\it* cap. 4.
\c 1
\cd With his praises he covertly exhorteth him not to be dismayed for his trouble, 6. (having grace given in Orders to help him, 8. and knowing for what cause he is persecuted) and namely with the example of Onesiphorus.
\p
\v 1 \sc Paul\sc* an Apostle of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ by the will of God, according to the promise of the life which is in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*:
\v 2 to Timothee my dearest son, grace, mercy, peace from God the Father, and Christ \sc Jesus\sc* our Lord.
\v 3 I give thanks to God, whom I serve from my progenitors in a pure conscience, that without intermission I have a memory of thee in my prayers, night and day.
\v 4 desiring to see thee, mindful of thy tears, that I may be filled with joy,
\v 5 calling to mind that faith which is in thee not feigned, which also dwelt first in thy grand-mother Lois, and thy mother Eunice, and I am sure that in thee also. \ef - \fr 1.5 \fq In thy grandmother. \ft ¹ Though God shew mercy to many that be of incredulous, heretical or ill parents, yet it is a goodly benediction of God to have good education and to have good faithful progenitors and Catholic parents. And it is a great sin to forsake the faith of our fathers that be Catholics, or contrary to our education in the Church to follow strange doctrines, abandoning not only our next natural parent's faith, but the ancient faith and belief of all our progenitors for many hundred years together. And if to follow the faith of mother and grandmother only, the Christian religion being then but newly planted, was so commendable even in a Bishop, how much more is it now laudable to cleave fast to the faith of so many our progenitors and ages that continued in the same Christian religion which they first received? ² Our Protestants in their great wisdom laugh at good simple men when they talk of their father's faith. But S. Hierom ³, \+it I am a Christian,\+it* saith he, \+it and born of Christian parents, and carry the sign of the cross in my forehead\+it*. And again ep. 65. c. 3. \+it Until this day the Christian world hath been without this doctrine, that faith will I hold fast being an old man, wherein I was born a child.\+it* And the holy Scriptures set us often to school to our fathers. ⁴ \+it Ask thy fathers, and they will shew thee, thy ancestors, and they will tell thee.\+it* And again, ⁵ \+it Our fathers have shewed unto us\+it*. And commonly the true God is called the God of the faithful and of their forefathers. \+it Dan. 2. 3.\+it* And false gods and new doctrines or opinions be named, \+it New and fresh, such as their fathers worshipped not. Deut. 32.\+it* Finally S. Paul both here and ⁶ often else allegeth for his defence and commendation, that he was of faithful progenitors. And it is a case that Heretics cannot lightly brag of, no one sect commonly during so long without intermission, that they can have many progenitors of the said sect. Which is a demonstration that their faith is not true, and that it is impossible our Catholic faith to be false, supposing the Christian religion to be true. \fq ¹ \ft A great blessing, to have Catholic progenitors: and very commendable to cleave fast to their faith. \fq ² \ft The people's speeches of their father's faith, is very Christian and laudable. \fq ³ \ft \+it Apol. cont. Ruff. li. 1. c. 8.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Deut. 32.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it & Ps. 43.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it * Act. 24. 2. Cor. 11.\+it*\ef*
\v 6 For the which cause I admonish thee that thou resuscitate the \f 1 \fr 1.6 \ft Here again it is plain that holy Orders give grace, and that even by and in the external ceremony of imposing the Bishop's hands. And it is a manner of speech specially used in this Apostle and S. Luke, that Orders give grace to the ordered, & that to take orders or authority to minister Sacraments or preach, is, to be given or delivered to God's grace. \+it Act. 14, 25.\+it*\f* grace of God, which is in thee by the imposition of my hands.
\v 7 For God hath not given us the spirit of fear: but of power, and love, and sobriety.
\v 8 Be not therefore ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner: but travail with the Gospel according to the power of God,
\v 9 who hath delivered and called us by his holy calling, \x - \xt Tit. 3, 5.\x* not according to our works, but according to his purpose and grace, which was given to us in Christ \sc Jesus\sc* \x - \xt Tit. 1, 3.\x* before the secular times.
\v 10 But it is manifested now by the illumination of our Saviour \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, who hath destroyed death, and illuminated life and incorruption by the Gospel:
\v 11 wherein \x - \xt 1. Timo. 2, 7.\x* I am appointed a preacher and Apostle and Master of the Gentiles.
\v 12 For the which cause also I suffer these things: but I am not confounded. For I know whom I have believed, and I am sure that he is able to keep my \it depositum\it* unto that day. \ef - \fr 1.12 \fq Depositum. \ft ¹ A great comfort to all Christians, that every of their good deeds and sufferings for Christ, and all the worldly losses sustained for defence or confession of their faith, be extant with God, and kept as \+it depositum\+it*, to be repayed or received again in heaven. Which if the worldlings believed or considered, they would not so much marvel to see Catholic men so willingly to lose land, liberty, credit, life and all for Christ's sake and the Church's faith. \fq ¹ \ft All our good deeds are laid up with God, to be rewarded.\ef*
\v 13 Have thou a form of sound words, which thou hast heard of me in faith and \f 1 \fr 1.13 \ft c Faith and love coupled commonly together in this Apostle's writings.\f*\f 2 \fr 1.13 \ft \+it c ἀγάπῃ τῇ ἐν Χριςῷ\+it*\f* in the love of Christ \sc Jesus\sc*. \ef - \fr 1.13 \fq A form. \ft The Apostles did set down a platform of faith, doctrine, & phrase of Catholic speech and preaching, & that not so much by writing (as here we see) as by word of mouth: to which he referreth Timothee over and above his Epistles unto him. ¹ And how precisely Christian Doctors ought to keep the form of words anciently appropriated to the mysteries and matters of our religion, S. Augustine expresseth in these words \+it li. 10 de civit. c. 23. Philosophers speak with freedom of words &c. but we must speak according to a certain rule, lest licentious liberty of words breed an impious opinion of the things also that are signified by the same\+it*. Trinity, person, essence, Consubstantial, Transubstantiation, Mass, Sacrament, and such like, be \+it verba sana\+it* (as the Apostle speaketh) \+it sound words\+it*, given to express certain high truths in religion, partly by the Apostles and first founders of our religion under Christ, and partly very aptly invented by holy Councels and fathers, to express as near as could be the high ineffable or unspeakable verity of some points, and to stop the Heretics' audacity and invention of new words and profane speeches in such things, which the Apostle warneth Timothee to avoid \+it 1 ep. c. 6. 20.\+it* and \+it 2 ep. 2, 16.\+it* See the Annotations there. \fq ¹ \ft We must speak in Catholic terms, after a certain rule of faith, and form of words.\ef*
\v 14 Keep the good \it depositum\it* by the Holy Ghost, which dwelleth in us.
\v 15 Thou knowest this, that all which are in Asia, be averted from me: of whom is Phigelus and Hermogenes.
\v 16 Our Lord give mercy to \x - \xt 2. Tim. 4, 19.\x* the house of Onesiphorus: because he hath often refreshed me, and hath \f 2 \fr 1.16 \ft What a happy & meritorious things it is to relieve the afflicted for religion, & not to be ashamed of their disgrace, irons, or what miseries so ever.\f* not been ashamed of my chain:
\v 17 but when he was come to Rome, he sought me carefully, and found me.
\v 18 Our Lord grant him to find mercy of our Lord in that day. And how many things he ministered to me at Ephesus, thou knowest better. \ef - \fr 1.18 \fq Our Lord. \ft ¹ To have this prayer of an Apostle, or any Priest or poor Cath. man so relieved, giveth the greatest hope at the day of our death or general judgement, that can be: and it is worth all the lauds, honours, and riches of the world. \fq ¹ \ft Relievers of Cath. prisoners.\ef*
\c 2
\cd He exhorteth him to labour diligently in his office, considering the reward in Christ, and his denial of them that deny him. 14. Not to contend, but to shun heretics: neither to be moved to see some subverted, considering that the elect continue Catholics, and that in the Church be of all sorts. 24. Yet with all sweetness to reclaim the deceived.
\p
\v 1 \sc Thou\sc* therefore my son, be strong in the grace which is in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*:
\v 2 and the things which thou hast heard of me by many witnesses, these commend to faithful men, which shall be fit to teach others also.
\v 3 Labour thou as a good soldier of Christ \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 4 No man being a soldier to God, entangleth himself with secular businesses; that he may please him to whom he hath approved himself. \ef - \fr 2.4 \fq No man being a soldier. \ft ¹ First of all, the Apostle ( \+it 1 Cor. 7.\+it* ) maketh marriage & the needful cares, solicitude, and distractions thereupon ever depending, special impediments of all such as should employ themselves wholly to God's service, as Bishops & Priests are bound to do. \+it He that is with a wife\+it* (saith he) \+it is careful for the world, how to please his wife, and is distracted or divided.\+it* 1 Cor. 7. Secondly, the practice of Physic, merchandise, or any other profane faculty and trade of life to gather riches, and much more to be given to hunting, hawking, gaming, shews, interludes, or the like pastimes, is here forbidden. ² Thirdly, the services of Princes and manifold base offices done to them for to obtain dignities and promotions, are disagreeable to Priestly functions. Not so, to be their chaplains for this purpose to preach unto them, to hear their confessions, to minister the Sacraments unto them, to say Divine service before them, and such other spiritual duties. For, all such services done to principal persons both of the Clergy and Laity, be godly and consonant to Priestly vocation. As also serving of Princes and Commonweals in civil causes and matters of state, in making peace and quietness among the people, by deciding or compounding their controversies, and all such like affairs tending to the honour of God and good of men, and to the upholding of true religion, when they may be done without notorious damage or hinderance of their spiritual charge, or when the hurts thereof be aboundantly recompensed by the necessary duties done for the general good of kingdom or Country: all such things (I say) be lawful and often very requisite. ³ And S. Augustine, S. Ambrose, S. Bernard, and other holy Bishops of old were much occupied therein, as we see in S. Augustine's book \+it de opere Monachorum c. 29. & Possid. in vit. c. 19.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft What secular affairs do not agree nor consist with spiritual men's function. \fq ² \ft How spiritual men may serve secular Princes, & deal in civil causes. \fq ³ \ft \+it in vit. S. Ambr. & Bern.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 For he also that striveth for the mastery, is not crowned unless he strive lawfully.
\v 6 The husband-man that laboureth, must first take of the fruits.
\v 7 Understand what I say: for our Lord will give thee in all things understanding.
\v 8 \f 1 \fr 2.8 \ft b Part of the Epistle upon S. George's day. April. 25. The rest is pa. 541.\f* Be mindful that our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ is risen again from the dead, of the seed of David, according to my Gospel,
\v 9 wherein I labour even unto bands, as a malefactor: but the word of God is not tied.
\v 10 Therefore \f 1 \fr 2.10 \ft Mark here that the elect (though sure of salvation) yet are saved by means of their preachers & teachers, as also by their own endeavours.\f* I sustain all things for the elect, that they also may obtain the salvation, which is in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*, with heavenly glory.
\v 11 A faithful saying. For if we be dead with him, we shall live also together.
\v 12 If we shall sustain, we shall also reign together. \x - \xt Mat. 10.\x* If we shall deny, he also will deny us.
\v 13 \x - \xt Ro. 3, 3.\x* If we believe not; he continueth faithful, he cannot deny himself.
\v 14 These things admonish: testifying before our Lord. Contend not in words, for it is profitable for nothing, but for the subversion of them that hear.
\v 15 Carefully provide to present thyself approved to God, a work-man not to be confounded, rightly handling the word of truth. \ef - \fr 2.15 \fq Rightly. \ft ¹ The Scriptures or challenge of the word of God is common to Catholics and Heretics, but all is in the handling of them, these later handle them guilefully, adultering the word of God, as ² elsewhere the Apostle speaketh: the other sincerely after the manner of the Apostles and doctors of God's Church. Which the Greek expresseth by a significant word of cutting a thing straight by a line, \+it ὀρθοτομοῦντα\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Catholics only, right handlers of the Scripture. \fq ² \ft \+it 2. Cor. 2. & 4.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 But \f 1 \fr 2.16 \ft c See the Annotations before \+it 1. Tim. 6. v. 20.\+it*\f* profane and vain speeches avoid: for they do much grow to impiety:
\v 17 and their speech spreadeth as a canker: of whom is Hymenæus and Philetus: \ef - \fr 2.17 \fq Their speech. \ft ¹ The speeches, preachings, and writings of Heretics be pestiferous, contagious, and creeping like a canker. Therefore Christian men must never hear their sermons nor read their books. For such men have a popular way of talk whereby the unlearned, and specially women loaden with sin, are easily beguiled. \+it Nothing is so easy\+it* (saith S. Hierom) \+it as with voluble and rolling tongue to deceive the rude people, which admire whatsoever they understand not. Ep. 2. ad Nepot. c. 10.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Heretical books and sermons are to be avoided.\ef*
\v 18 who have erred from the truth, saying that the resurrection is done already, and have subverted the faith of some.
\v 19 But the sure foundation of God standeth, having this seal, Our Lord knoweth, who be his, and let every one depart from iniquity that nameth the name of our Lord.
\v 20 But in a great house there are not only vessels of gold and of silver, but also of wood and of earth: and certain indeed unto honour, but certain unto contumely. \ef - \fr 2.20 \fq In a great house \ft ¹ He meaneth not that Hymenæus and Philetus (of whom he spake immediately before) or other heretics, be properly within the Church, as Catholic men are, though grievous sinners: but that evil men who for the punishment of their sins become heretics, were before they fell from their faith as vessels of contumely, within the Church. Yea and often also after they be severed in heart and in the sight of God, so long as they stand in external profession and use of the same Sacraments, and in the outward fellowship of Catholics, not yet either separated of themselves, nor cast out by the governors of the Church, so long (we say) they be after a sort in the Church: though properly and indeed they be out of the compass of God's house. Mary of those that are openly severed in Sacraments, Service, and communion, there is no question but they are out of the Church. \fq ¹ \ft Who are out of the Church or within it.\ef*
\v 21 If any man therefore shall cleanse himself from these, he shall be a vessel unto honour, sanctified & profitable to our Lord, prepared to every good work. \ef - \fr 2.21 \fq Cleanse himself. \ft ¹ Man then hath free-will to make himself a vessel of salvation or damnation: though salvation be attributed to God's mercy principally, the other to his just judgement: neither of both being repugnant to our free-will, but working with and by the same, all such effects in us as to his providence and our deserts be agreeable. \fq ¹ \ft Free-will.\ef*
\v 22 But youthful desires fly, and pursue justice, faith, charity, and peace with them that invocate our Lord from a pure heart.
\v 23 And \x - \xt Tit. 3, 9.\x* foolish and unlearned questions avoid, knowing that they engender brawls.
\v 24 But the servant of our Lord must not wrangle: but be mild toward all men, apt to teach, patient,
\v 25 with modesty admonishing them that resist the truth: lest sometime \f 1 \fr 2.25 \ft Conversion from sin and heresy is the gift of God and of his special grace: yet here we see, good exhortations and prayer and such other helps of man be profitable thereunto. Which could not be, if we had not free will.\f* God give them repentance to know the truth:
\v 26 and they recover themselves from the snares of the devil, of whom they are held captive at his will.
\c 3
\cd He prophesieth of Heretics to come. 6. and noteth certain then also for such, bidding him to avoid them, 10. and (what so ever persecution befall for it) to continue constant in the Catholic doctrine, both because of his Master (S. Paul himself) 15. and also because of his own knowledge in the Scriptures.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* this know thou, that \x - \xt 1. Tim. 4, 1.\x* in the last days shall approach perilous times.
\v 2 And men shall be lovers of themselves, covetous, haughty, proud, blasphemous, not obedient to their parents, unkind, wicked, \ef - \fr 3.2 \fq Men shall be. \ft All these words S. Cyprian expoundeth of such as by pride and disobedience resist God's Priests. Let no faithful man, saith he, that keepeth in mind our Lord's and the Apostle's admonition, marvel if he see in the later times some proud and stubborn fellows and the enemies of God's Priests, go out of the Church or impugn the same: when both our Lord and the Apostle foretold us that such should be. \+it Cypr. ep. 55. nu. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 without affection, without peace, accusers, incontinent, unmerciful, without benignity,
\v 4 traitors, stubborn, puffed up, and lovers of voluptuousness more than of God:
\v 5 having an appearance indeed of piety, but denying the virtue thereof. And these avoid.
\v 6 For of these be they that craftily enter into houses; & lead captive seely women laden with sins, which are led with diverse desires: \ef - \fr 3.6 \fq Women loaden. \ft ¹ Women loaden with sins, are for such their deservings, and through the frailty of their sex, more subject to the heretics' deceits, than men: the enemy attempting (as he did in the fall of our first parents) by them to overthrow men. See S. Hierom upon the 3. chapter of Jeremie. Where he addeth that every heresy is first broached \+it propter gulam & ventrem\+it*, for gluttony and belly-cheer. \fq ¹ \ft Women easily seduced by heresy.\ef*
\v 7 always learning, and never attaining to the knowledge of the truth.
\v 8 But as \x - \xt Exo. 7.\x* Iannes and Mambres \f 2 \fr 3.8 \ft That those Magicians which resisted Moyses, were thus called, it is not written in all the old Testament, therefore it came to the Apostle's knowledge by tradition, as the Church now hath the names of the 3 kings, of the penitent thief, of the soldier that pierced Christ's side on the Cross, and of the like.\f* resisted Moyses, so these also resist the truth, men corrupted in mind, reprobate concerning the faith.
\v 9 But they shall prosper no further: for their folly shall be manifest to all, as theirs also was. \ef - \fr 3.9 \fq Folly manifest. \ft ¹ All heretics in the beginning seem to have some shew of truth, God for just punishment of men's sins permitting them for some while in some persons and places to prevail: but in short time God detecteth them, and openeth the eyes of men to see their deceits: in so much that after the first brunt they be maintained by force only, all wise men in a manner seeing their falsehood, though for troubling the state of such common-weals where unluckily they have been received, they cannot be so suddenly extirped. \fq ¹ \ft The folly of Heretics in time appeareth.\ef*
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 3.10 \ft b The rest of the Epist. for S. George's day.\f* But thou hast attained to my doctrine, institution, purpose, faith, longanimity, love, patience,
\v 11 persecutions, passions: what manner of things were done to me at Antioch, at I conium, at Lystra: what manner of persecutions I sustained. And out of all our Lord delivered me.
\v 12 And all that will live godly in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*, shall suffer persecution. \ef - \fr 3.12 \fq All that will live. \ft ¹ All holy men suffer one kind of persecution or other, being grieved and molested by the wicked, one way or another: but not all that suffer persecution, be holy, as all malefactors. The Church and Catholic Princes persecute heretics, and be persecuted of them again, as S. Augustine often declareth. \+it See ep. 48.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Persecution.\ef*
\v 13 But evil men & seducers shall prosper to the worse: erring, and driving into error. \ef - \fr 3.13 \fq Prosper. \ft Though heresies and the authors of them be after a while discovered & by little and little forsaken generally of the honest, discreet, and men careful of their own salvation, yet their authors and other great sinners proceed from one error and heresy to another, and finally to plain Atheism and all Devilish disorder.\ef*
\v 14 But thou, \f 1 \fr 3.14 \ft In all danger and diversity of false sects, S. Paul's admonition is, ever to abide in that was first taught and delivered, never to give over our old faith for a new fancy. This is it which before he calleth \+it depositum. 1. Tim. 6. and 2. Tim. 1.\+it*\f* continue in those things which thou hast learned, & are committed to thee: knowing of whom thou hast learned;
\v 15 & because from thine infancy thou hast known the holy Scriptures, which can instruct thee to salvation, by the faith that is in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 16 \x - \xt 2. Pet. 1, 21.\x* All Scripture inspired of God, is profitable to teach, to argue, to correct, to instruct in justice: \ef - \fr 3.16 \fq All Scripture. \ft ¹ Besides the Apostles' teaching and tradition, the reading of holy Scriptures is a great defence and help of the faithful, and specially of a Bishop, not only to avoid and condemn all heresies, but to the guiding of a man in all justice, good life and works. Which commendation is not here given to the books of the new Testament only (whereof he here speaketh not, as being yet for a great part not written) but to the Scripture of the old Testament also, yea and to every book of it. For there is not one of them, nor any part of them, but it is profitable to the end aforesaid, if it be read and understood according to the same Spirit wherewith it was written. \fq ¹ \ft The great profit of reading the Scriptures.\ef* \ef - \fr 3.16 \ft ¹ The Heretics upon this commendation of holy Scriptures, pretend (very simply in good sooth) that therefore nothing is necessary to justice and salvation but Scriptures. As though every thing that is profitable or necessary to any effect, excluded all other help, and were only enough to attain the same. By which reason a man might as well prove that the old Testament were enough, and so exclude the new: or any one piece of all the old, and thereby exclude the rest. For he affirmeth every Scripture to have the foresaid utilities. and they might see in the very next line before, that he requireth his constant perseverance in the doctrine which he had taught him over and about that he had learned out of the Scriptures of the old Testament, which he had read from his infancy, but could not thereby learn all the mysteries of Christian religion therein. Neither doth the Apostle affirm here that he had his knowledge of Scriptures, by reading only, without help of masters and teachers, as the Adversaries hereupon (to commit the holy Scriptures to every man's presumption) do gather: but affirmeth only that Timothee knew the Scriptures and therefore had studied them by hearing good readers and teachers, as S. Paul himself did of Gamaliel and the like, and as all christian students do, that be trained up from their youth in Catholic universities in the study of Divinity. \fq ¹ \ft The Heretics' foolish argument: All Scripture is profitable, ergo only Scripture is necessary & sufficient.\ef*
\v 17 that the man of God may be perfect, instructed to every good work.
\c 4
\cd He requireth him to be earnest while he may, because the time will come when they will not abide Catholic preaching, 5. and to fulfil his course, as himself now hath done. 9. and to come unto him with speed, because the rest of his train are dispersed, and he draweth now to heaven.
\p
\v 1 I \f 1 \fr 4.1 \ft The Epistle for holy Doctors, and for S. Silvester. Decemb. \+it 31.\+it* & for S. Hilary, Ian. \+it 14.\+it* and S. Dominic \+it August 4.\+it*\f* Testify before God and \sc Jesus\sc* Christ who shall judge the living and the dead, and by his advent, and his Kingdom:
\v 2 Preach the word. Urge in season, out of season, reprove, beseech, rebuke, in all patience and doctrine.
\v 3 For there shall be a time when they will not bear sound doctrine: but according to their own desires they will heap to themselves masters, having itching ears, \ef - \fr 4.3 \fq There shall be a time. \ft ¹ If ever this time come (as needs it must that the Apostle foresaw and foretold) now it is undoubtedly, for the properties fall so just in every point upon our new Masters and their Disciples, that they may seem to be pourtered out, rather than prophesied of. Never were there such delicate Doctors that could so pleasantly claw and so sweetly rub the itching ears of their hearers, as these, which have a doctrine framed for every man's fancy, lust, liking and desire. the people not so fast crying, ² \+it speak placentia, things that please\+it*: but the Masters as fast warranting them to do \+it placentia\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The Apostle prophesied of our new delicate preachers. \fq ² \ft \+it Esa. 30. v. 10.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 and from the truth certes they will avert their hearing, and to fables they will be converted.
\v 5 But be thou vigilant, labour in all things, do the work of an Evangelist, fulfill thy ministry. Be sober.
\v 6 For I am even now \f 1 \fr 4.6 \ft The martyrdom of Saints is so acceptable to God, that it is counted as it were a sacrifice in his sight, and therefore hath many effects both in the party that suffereth it, and in others that are partakers of the merit as of a Sacrifice: which name it hath by a Metaphor.\f* to be sacrificed: and the time of my resolution is at hand.
\v 7 I have fought a good fight, I have consummate my course, I have kept the faith.
\v 8 Concerning the rest, there is laid up for me a crown of justice, which our Lord will render to me in that day, a just judge: and not only to me, but to them also that love his coming. \ef - \fr 4.8 \fq A crown of justice. \ft ¹ This place convinceth for the Catholics, that all good works done by God's grace after the first justification, be truly and properly meritorious, and fully worthy of everlasting life: and that thereupon heaven is the due and just stipend, crown, or recompense, which God by his justice oweth to the persons so working by his grace. for he rendereth or repayeth heaven as a just judge, and not only as a merciful giver. and the crown which he payeth, is not only of mercy or favour or grace, but also of justice ². It is his merciful favour and grace, that we work well & merit heaven: it is his justice, for those merits to give us a crown correspondent in heaven. S. Augustine upon these words of the Apostle, expresseth both briefly thus, \+it How should he repay as a just judge, unless he had first given as a merciful father?\+it* Li. de grat. & lib. arbit. c. 6. \fq ¹ \ft Works meritorious. \fq ² \ft How heaven is due both of justice and mercy.\ef* \ef - \fr 4.8 \ft And when you hear or read any thing in the Scriptures, that may seem to derogate from man's works in this case, it is always meant of works considered in their own nature and value, not implying the grace of Christ, by which grace it cometh, & not of the work in it-self that we have a right to heaven & deserve it worthily ¹: which the Apostle in the 6. to the Hebrews more then insinuateth, saying these words, \+it God is not unjust, to forget your work & love which you have shewed in his name &c.\+it* As though he would say, that he were unjust if he did forget to recompense their works. ² The parable also of the men sent into the vineyard, proveth that heaven is our own right, bargained for and wrought for, and accordingly payed unto us as our hire at the day of judgement, for that is \+it merces\+it* and \+it μισθὸς\+it* whereby the Scripture so often calleth it. It is the goal, the mark, the price, the hire, of all striving, running, labouring, due both by promise and by covenant and right debt ³. See a notable place in S. Augustine in \+it Psal. 83 in fine:\+it* and \+it 100 in initio. & ho. 14. c. 2. ii. 50. hom.\+it* S. Cyprian also, and namely the later end of his book \+it de opere & eleemosyna:\+it* and thou shalt easily contemn the contrary falsehood, which doth not so much derogate from man's works, as from God's grace which is the cause and ground of all worthiness in man's merits. S. Augustine's words ⁴ be these, \+it Mark that he to whom our Lord gave grace, hath our Lord also his debter. He found him a giver, in the time of mercy: he hath him his debter in the time of judgement\+it*. See the place and the rest here quoted, where he examineth and explicateth the matter at large. \fq ¹ \ft It is not of us, but of God's grace, that works be meritorious. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. 20.\+it* \fq ³ \ft To such good works heaven is due: to say the contrary, is to derogate from God's grace. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it In Ps. 100.\+it*\ef*
\v 9 Make haste to come to me quickly.
\v 10 For Demas hath left me, loving this world, and is gone to Thessalonica: Crescens into Galatia, Titus into Dalmatia.
\v 11 \x - \xt Col. 4, 14.\x* Luke only is with me. Take Mark, and bring him with thee: for he is profitable to me for the ministry.
\v 12 But Tychicus I have sent to Ephesus.
\v 13 The cloak that I left at Troas with Carpus, coming bring with thee, and the books, especially the parchment.
\v 14 Alexander the Coppersmith hath shewed me much evil: our Lord will reward him according to his works:
\v 15 whom do thou also avoid, for he hath greatly resisted our words.
\v 16 In my first answer no man was with me, but all did forsake me: be it not imputed to them.
\v 17 But our Lord stood to me, and strengthened me, that by me the preaching may be accomplished, and all Gentiles may hear: and I was delivered from the mouth of the lion.
\v 18 Our Lord \f 1 \fr 4.18 \fq hath delivered \ft \+it will deliver\+it*\f* hath delivered me from all evil work: and will save me unto his heavenly Kingdom. To whom be glory for ever and ever. Amen.
\v 19 Salute Prisca and Aquila, and \x - \xt 2. Timo. 1, 16.\x* the house of Onesiphorus.
\v 20 Erastus remained at Corinth. And Trophimus I left sick at Miletum.
\v 21 Make haste to come before winter. Eubulus and Pudens and \f 1 \fr 4.21 \ft c This Linus was coadjutor with & under S. Peter, & so counted second in the number of Popes.\f* Linus and Claudia, and all the brethren, salute thee.
\v 22 Our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ be with thy spirit. Grace be with you. Amen.
